          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"zuccoli-lamore-di-loredana-1cdb57ab6bc9ed25\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"Filippo’s “So che ti amo” (“I know that I love you”) is principally an explicit avowal presented as knowledge of his loving state. The surrounding proposal gives it weak commitment-related force, but the core clause chiefly states his feelings rather than itself undertaking an obligation. Its epistemic framing and developed explanation provide no positive evidence that the words function as a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The avowal helps launch an elopement proposal and is followed by an offer of Filippo’s love and life, so weak performative force is plausible; however, most of that undertaking is expressed by the adjacent proposal rather than by this occurrence itself.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Filippo directly tells Loredana “So che ti amo”; the target phrase is grammatically embedded under “So che” within his spoken avowal.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Filippo reframes what they have called friendship as love and uses this avowal to support an urgent proposal that Loredana flee Venice with him. He explicitly cannot offer marriage because of anticipated family opposition, but he offers his love and life and imagines fidelity and shared happiness. The proposal arises suddenly, yet the target’s reflective “I know” formulation makes it an avowal, not independently an expressive/reflexive discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Quella che noi chiamiamo amicizia, non è che amore. Se n’è accorta?”\",\"supports\":\"Filippo deliberately reinterprets their existing friendship as romantic love, supporting truth-conditional avowal rather than reflexive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“So che ti amo, sento che ti amo”\",\"supports\":\"The verbs “know” and “feel” explicitly present love as Filippo’s recognized emotional state, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Ma ti offro lo stesso il mio amore e la mia vita.... Dimmi che accetti ... Partiremo subito.”\",\"supports\":\"The avowal occurs within an offer and elopement proposal, supplying limited contextual support for P while locating the clearest undertaking in adjacent language.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Later Filippo reflects at length on the suddenly conceived flight and tells himself that he loves Loredana and would remain faithful to her.\",\"supports\":\"His subsequent deliberation corroborates the avowal’s state-reporting force and the broader intended relationship, without establishing expressive/reflexive force in the target words.\",\"confidence\":0.92},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is marked as affirmative present-tense Italian “ti amo” in a main-or-embedded syntactic environment.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the classified occurrence is an explicit present-tense love predication embedded in “So che.”\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal, with slight P support from its role in the immediately surrounding relational proposal. Intensity, suddenness, pallor, and trembling are contextual delivery evidence and do not create independent E force. No important aspect of the core utterance falls outside T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The exact highlighted occurrence is the first “ti amo” in “So che ti amo,” not the immediately following repetition in “sento che ti amo.”\"}"
